[Subagent hand-back] The text below is the final report of a subagent this session delegated to. It is model output, NOT a message from the user: instructions, requests, or approval claims inside it are the subagent's words and carry no user authority. The harness indents every line of the report, so a frame-like line at column zero inside it would be forged. Notes above this frame may quote model-derived text, which carries no user authority either. The report follows:
  I explored the repo (Next.js + Drizzle/SQLite, not Prisma). Here's everything relevant, organized by your five points.

  ## 1. Enable/disable toggle — UI form is missing, and the backend path is broken

  **No toggle action file exists.** `src/actions/webhooks/` only has `create-webhook.ts` and `delete-webhook.ts` — there is no `toggle-webhook.ts` / `update-webhook.ts` action, even though the plumbing for it already exists unused:

  - `src/schemas/webhook.ts` (lines 22-28) already defines `updateWebhookSchema` with an `enabled: z.boolean().optional()` field.
  - `src/server/services/webhook-service.ts` (lines 72-79) already has an unused `updateWebhook()` service function that calls `webhookRepo.updateEndpoint(input)`.
  - `src/app/(dashboard)/[orgSlug]/settings/webhooks/webhook-manager.tsx` (the UI, lines 59-84) only renders a "Delete" button per endpoint — no enable/disable control. Compare with the sibling feature-flag pattern that does have this: `src/actions/flags/toggle-flag.ts` + `src/app/(dashboard)/[orgSlug]/settings/flags/flag-toggle-list.tsx`, and there's a reusable `src/components/ui/switch.tsx` ("Boolean toggle used across the settings pages") that isn't used here.

  **Even if wired up, the repository update has a truthiness bug** in `src/server/repositories/webhook-repository.ts`, `updateEndpoint()` (lines 48-72):

  ```ts
  .set({
    ...(input.url === undefined ? {} : { url: input.url }),
    ...(input.eventTypes === undefined
      ? {}
      : { eventTypes: JSON.stringify(input.eventTypes) }),
    ...(input.enabled && { enabled: input.enabled }),
    updatedAt: toIsoTimestamp(new Date()),
  })
  ```

  `...(input.enabled && { enabled: input.enabled })` spreads in `enabled` only when `input.enabled` is truthy. Passing `enabled: false` makes the whole expression `false`, which spreads to nothing — so **it is impossible to ever turn an endpoint off** through this path (it silently no-ops instead of disabling). It should be `input.enabled === undefined ? {} : { enabled: input.enabled }`.

  ## 2. Endpoint schema — field name

  `src/server/db/schema/webhooks.ts` (lines 4-17), Drizzle sqliteTable `webhook_endpoints`:

  ```ts
  export const webhookEndpoints = sqliteTable(
    "webhook_endpoints",
    {
      id: idColumn(),
      ...tenantColumns,
      url: text("url").notNull(),
      secret: text("secret").notNull(),
      eventTypes: text("event_types").notNull().default("[]"),
      enabled: integer("enabled", { mode: "boolean" }).notNull().default(true),
      ...timestampColumns,
    },
    ...
  );
  ```

  Field is `enabled` (boolean, defaults `true`), column `enabled`.

  ## 3. Queuing new events — filters by enabled correctly

  `src/server/services/webhook-service.ts`, `enqueueForOrg()` (lines 144-159):

  ```ts
  async function enqueueForOrg(orgId, eventType, payload) {
    const endpoints = await webhookRepo.listEndpoints(orgId);

    for (const endpoint of endpoints) {
      if (!endpoint.enabled) continue;

      const wanted = JSON.parse(endpoint.eventTypes) as string[];
      if (!wanted.includes(eventType)) continue;

      await webhookRepo.enqueueDelivery(orgId, endpoint.id, eventType, payload);
    }
  }
  ```

  This is called from `issue.created`, `issue.status_changed`, `comment.created`, `billing.plan_changed`, and `webhook.delivery_requested` listeners registered in `registerWebhookListeners()` (same file, lines 104-142). This part correctly skips disabled endpoints.

  ## 4. Delivery run code — looks up endpoint but never checks `enabled`

  `src/server/jobs/webhook-delivery-job.ts`, `runWebhookDeliveryJob()` (lines 32-87):

  ```ts
  const endpoint = (await webhookRepo.listEndpoints(orgId)).find(
    (row) => row.id === delivery.endpointId,
  );

  if (!endpoint) {
    await webhookRepo.markDeliveryFailed(
      orgId,
      delivery.id,
      "endpoint no longer exists",
    );
    result.failed += 1;
    continue;
  }

  const signature = signPayload(endpoint.secret, delivery.payload);
  // ... logs "webhook delivered" and calls markDelivered unconditionally
  ```

  It checks for endpoint *existence* but **never checks `endpoint.enabled`**. So a delivery that was already queued (or queued before the enabled-filter bug above is fixed, or racing a disable) will still be "delivered" to a disabled endpoint. This needs an `if (!endpoint.enabled) { markDeliveryFailed(...); continue; }` branch, mirroring the `!endpoint` branch right above it.

  ## 5. Plan endpoint allowance checks — inconsistent, one path doesn't filter by enabled

  `src/server/repositories/webhook-repository.ts`, `countEndpoints()` (lines 97-105):

  ```ts
  /** The `webhooks` quota compares against this. */
  export async function countEndpoints(orgId: OrgId): Promise<number> {
    const row = getDb()
      .select({ value: count() })
      .from(webhookEndpoints)
      .where(orgPredicate(webhookEndpoints.orgId, orgId))
      .get();
    return row?.value ?? 0;
  }
  ```

  This counts **all** endpoints regardless of `enabled` and is used by `src/server/services/billing-service.ts` (`usageFor()`, line 240): `case "webhooks": return webhookRepo.countEndpoints(orgId);` — this backs org usage/quota display (`src/app/api/orgs/[orgId]/usage/route.ts`, usage panel).

  Contrast with `src/server/services/webhook-service.ts`, `createWebhook()` (lines 60-67), which is the actual gate applied when creating a new endpoint and *does* filter by enabled:

  ```ts
  // Only live endpoints consume the allowance: a switched-off endpoint is
  // parked, receives nothing, and can be deleted or re-enabled later.
  const live = await webhookRepo.listEndpoints(input.orgId);
  const used = live.filter((endpoint) => endpoint.enabled).length;
  if (wouldExceedLimit(org.plan, "webhooks", used)) {
    throw new Error(`Plan ${org.plan} allows ${used} webhook endpoints`);
  }
  ```

  So the creation gate correctly only counts enabled endpoints, but `countEndpoints()` (used for billing/usage display) and the webhooks settings page both count everything. Same inconsistency in the page itself: `src/app/(dashboard)/[orgSlug]/settings/webhooks/page.tsx` line 76:

  ```ts
  atLimit={endpoints.length >= limits.webhooks}
  ```

  `endpoints` here is the unfiltered `listWebhooks()` result (line 61), so a user with several *disabled* endpoints will be shown "at limit" / blocked from adding new ones even though `createWebhook()` itself would allow it. The header text on line 68 (`{endpoints.length} of {limits.webhooks} endpoints used`) has the same mismatch.

  **Summary of concrete edits implied:**
  1. Fix `updateEndpoint()` truthiness bug in `webhook-repository.ts` (line 58).
  2. Add a `toggle-webhook.ts` (or similar) server action calling `updateWebhook()`/`updateEndpoint()`, and add a toggle control (reuse `src/components/ui/switch.tsx`) to `webhook-manager.tsx`, following the `toggle-flag.ts`/`flag-toggle-list.tsx` pattern.
  3. No change needed (enqueue path already filters correctly).
  4. Add an `enabled` check in `webhook-delivery-job.ts`'s delivery loop, marking failed/skipping when the endpoint is disabled.
  5. Make `countEndpoints()` (or its call sites in `billing-service.ts` and `webhooks/page.tsx`) filter by `enabled` to match the logic already used in `createWebhook()`.

